{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the standalone J4 room observer and its live capture wrapper, for request 5af22f40. Verdict: APPROVED, for one later planner-executed ordinary read of the existing room, and for nothing else.\n\nObserver PINS has SHA-256 2014adc6, and its entry `observe-room.mjs` has d8235974. Capture PINS has 6996105e, and its wrapper `run.py` has ad607d72. This approval covers those exact bytes. It releases no setup, finalizer, admission or operator action, and it claims no J4 or S1 progress.\n\nI read both programs, the receipt contract and the network helper. I ran no live read and supplied no private channel. The only live request I made was one public `GET /health`.\n\n## What holds\n\n- The route frontier is exact. `observeReads` compares URL, method and order for all eight requests: health, one session POST, five summaries and one Rules entry. The POST body must equal the retained signed request. There's no act, preparation, settle, provider, head, fork or Git route, and no retry path.\n- Live health matches what the observer expects. At 11:05 UTC, `GET /health` on `dap-demo` reported version da13242c, `register: ready`, `sessions: ok` and Artifacts wired. `worker.ts:490` answers a session with 200, as the wrapper requires.\n- The room checks are exact: five full refs and pins, the admin and both agents' member, key and controller rows, the Rules head, the published fact by hash, and the publish act's actor, kind, fields, expected revision and effect.\n- Failure projection is generic. The catch records only a fixed classification and stage, and no exception text reaches public output. The channel value is never parsed, hashed or emitted by the wrapper, and only the observer opens it. The public result holds public keys, refs and metadata only.\n- Environment forwarding is explicit: one `Popen` call receives the five ordinary variables and the opaque channel. The seam checks argv, cwd and the new session, and it restores `Popen` before the later `git` calls.\n- Nothing imports setup or private originals. The observer doesn't import the operator, recovery or admission code. It compares historical private paths as strings only, and the wrapper doesn't call the owner's halt helper.\n- Bootstrap checks hold. Before any helper import, the observer checks each helper and input against its reviewed digest, and it refuses the inactive template before it looks for a channel.\n- Prior evidence holds. The qualification unit has 120 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest e648ac69, no file is writable, the inventory digest is d084c127, and the receipt closes 0/0 with all seven cases passing. Both pin sets verify against their 19 and 14 files.\n\n## The one untested step, which I checked\n\nThe wrapper's `validate_observation` has never run, and a false refusal would cost this one-time read. So I ran it in a scratch copy on a value that I built from the real inputs, in the shape that `observeReads` and `main` produce. I compared that shape with the qualified `healthy-result.json`. It accepts the value.\n\nThese read-only checks also pass:\n\n- `parseEnrollInput` accepts the actual predecessor;\n- `sourceGuard` on the SDK checkout returns the exact `source` value the validator expects;\n- the checkout is clean at 112eff12;\n- `node_modules` is a real directory;\n- the lock stamp equals the lockfile digest.\n\nMy built value isn't a live reply, so a mismatch in a live value can still refuse. That refusal would fail closed.\n\n## Conditions for the run\n\n1. Confirm the approval ID. The wrapper checks only the form of `release.checkerApproval`, not that the act exists. Root and the planner should read this act back with `git show` before the run. It names no other approval.\n2. Inspect `stderr.log` before sealing or publishing the capture. An unhandled rejection in the child could print a path or a URL to stderr, and the wrapper retains stderr byte for byte. I found no such path in the code, but stderr is the one stream that the observer doesn't project.\n3. Treat the private run as secret after the read. `network` writes each scope response to it, so `002.response.bin` holds the 60-second session credential. The directory is owner-only and the credential expires, which is acceptable. Do not copy that directory into evidence.\n4. Take no retry. A failed read goes back to the planner with its capture kept, under a new release if one is needed.\n5. Later setup must prove what this read doesn't: agent key possession, agent sessions and private custody equality.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `run.py`'s error text still says \"Synthetic capture refused\" on a live refusal. It's cosmetic, and changing it would change the reviewed digest, so leave it for any later revision.\n2. The command record publishes `PATH` and `TMPDIR` values. They reveal local layout only.\n3. The periodic inventories can detect an overrun only after a write, as the plan says.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe observer stays separate from setup and reuses the maintained SDK and pure helpers, and no setup state moves. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nAn admin key signs one 60-second read session and no act. The route frontier allows no other request, and no private value reaches public output by any path I could find. Approved, with conditions 1 to 3.\n\n## Simplification\n\nAbout 35 KB of observer and wrapper is a lot for eight reads. The one-time release, custody separation and exact readback are the reason, and they follow the J4 packet's existing form. Rewriting them now would cost more than it saves. If J4 needs further reads of this kind, a shared read-only capture would be cheaper. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved, with conditions 1 to 3.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- This approval covers one later ordinary same-room read. It proves nothing about setup, providers, Native Task, exports or physical drain. Its outcome gets a separate review before any successor setup admission.\n\n## Correction to 82ff38c9\n\nMy clarification asked the admission check to confirm that the operator review's verdict \"is approved\". `gs state` refuses `body.verdict` on a report without Git artifacts, so a review of `/private/tmp` Source carries its verdict in its text, as this one does. The check should require the report's first line to read `Verdict: APPROVED` or to contain it, alongside the two digest lines. The other conditions stand.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ef5c2881d4607ecdca571fdfb2550df71e561681 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:043d587ef18b62fdeee6b57f8968de1567206697, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:070231aadcb17348891458bc2eedb5bbf529860c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:171f5f57d26458ff1e85c1f9e0a69fefb342bc9b and 19 more"}}